\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Proposition}
\title{Mixing of the field state and uniqueness of the vacuum}
\date{2026-11-09}

\p{In the coupling domain of \ref{fdip-00IC}, the invariant
state #{\omega} of \ref{fdip-00I6} is mixing for the translation
action on field observables: for all #{F,G\in L^2(\nu)},
##{\omega(F\,\alpha_aG)\longrightarrow\omega(F)\omega(G)
                             \qquad(|a|\to\infty),}
where #{\alpha_a\Phi(g)=\Phi(\tau_ag)} and #{\alpha_a} is the
induced measure-preserving pullback. In particular,
##{J(f+\tau_ag)\longrightarrow J(f)J(g)}
for real Schwartz #{f,g}. With the A-C properties of
\ref{fdip-00I8}, its Euclidean and reconstructed physical
vacua are unique.}

\proof{
\p{The finite laws satisfy FKG association by
\citet{Theorem V.3, p. 207}{guerra1975euclideanII}, with the
bounded even onsite weights checked in \ref{fdip-00IC}.
This inequality passes to the selected continuum and volume state
for bounded continuous increasing cylinder functions. Indeed, at a
fixed volume, convergence of second moments gives tightness for each
finite family of smears. Convergence of higher moments supplies
uniform integrability for every polynomial, so each subsequential
law has the limiting moments of \ref{fdip-00I4}. The continuum law
has finite exponential moments of linear smears, by
\citet{Corollary 3.14'}{frohlich1974schwingerI} and the exact law
identification in \ref{fdip-00I6}, and is therefore moment
determinate. Every subsequential law is the same, proving
finite-dimensional weak convergence. The volume limit follows from
the characteristic convergence of \ref{fdip-00I6}. Bounded
continuous testing now passes the FKG inequality.}

\p{For a cylinder calculation, explicitly choose the standard bases
in the finite spaces of arguments. Let #{X_i=\Phi(f_i)} and
#{Y_j=\Phi(g_j)} with nonnegative smears; take bounded smooth real
#{F,G} with bounded first derivatives. Set
##{c_i=\|\partial_iF\|_\infty,\quad
 d_j=\|\partial_jG\|_\infty,\quad
 A=\sum_ic_iX_i,\quad B=\sum_jd_jY_j.}
The functions #{A\pm F} and #{B\pm G} are coordinatewise
increasing. FKG for the two equal-sign pairs, added together, gives
#{\operatorname{Cov}(F,G)\ge-\operatorname{Cov}(A,B)}.
Adding the two opposite-sign inequalities gives the reverse upper
bound. Thus
##{|\operatorname{Cov}(F(X),G(Y))|
 \le\sum_{i,j}c_id_j\operatorname{Cov}(X_i,Y_j).}
For the unbounded linear terms first use increasing clipping of
#{A\pm F} and #{B\pm G}, then pass in #{L^2} by their finite
second moments. This justifies all 4 FKG applications.}

\p{The decay of \ref{fdip-00ID} makes the right side tend to zero
under translation. Signed smears are differences of nonnegative
ones; real and imaginary parts cover complex cylinders. This
includes the exponentials of compact smooth smeared fields. For a
Schwartz test #{f}, choose compact smooth #{f_R\to f} in Schwartz
topology. Then
##{\|\ue^{\ui \Phi(f)}-\ue^{\ui \Phi(f_R)}\|_2^2
                   =2-2\operatorname{Re}J(f-f_R)\longrightarrow0.}
Invariance makes this approximation uniform after translation.
Cylinder exponentials span a dense subspace of the field #{L^2}
space; their density follows from uniqueness of finite-dimensional
Fourier transforms and the cylinder sigma-algebra. Cauchy-Schwarz
and invariant #{L^2} approximation extend mixing to all #{L^2}
observables.}

\p{In particular #{J} satisfies condition (4.1) of
\citek{frohlich1974verification}. The general Proposition D,
pp. 303-304, applies together with the A-C verification in
\ref{fdip-00I8}; it gives both Euclidean and physical vacuum
uniqueness. The separate nonzero-external-field theorem is not used.}
}
